file	key	english	chinese
Magister_CIV4GameText_FFH2.xml	TXT_KEY_RELIGION_ANOINTED_PEDIA	The Anointed are a cult of psychopaths devoted to Aeron, the God of Body, Strength, Sadism, Lust, Rage, Hate, and especially premeditated Murder. All of its members enjoy violence, and view killing or mutilating the innocent as a most sacred act. Although Aeron is the patron of Berserkers, he prefers the more subtle arts of the most cold hearted assassins. The cult is dominated by small group of powerful warriors called The Occisors. There is not much structure in this religion, although its members are encouraged to attain elite positions in other evil organizations. Aeron is best known as the Lord of Murder. His worshipers are usually terrifyingly powerful warriors, who could easily win most fair fights but pride themselves in never fighting fair. They prefer to strike the killing blows before their targets realize they are enemies. Aeron is also known as Maiden Friend; he has a great weakness for physical beauty, which his worshipers often inherit. They are often unwilling to harm those of great beauty. [PARAGRAPH:2]State religion allows access to:[PARAGRAPH:1][TAB][LINK=UNIT_DANAG]Danag the Faithful[\\LINK] (world unit)[PARAGRAPH:1][TAB][LINK=UNIT_SIR_HALEWYN]Sir Halewyn[\\LINK] (world unit)[PARAGRAPH:1][TAB][LINK=UNIT_LUIDAG]Luidag the Rag[\\LINK] (world unit)[PARAGRAPH:1][TAB]Halves the production cost of: [PARAGRAPH:1][TAB][LINK=BUILDING_HUNTING_LODGE]Hunting Lodges[\\LINK] [PARAGRAPH:1][TAB][LINK=BUILDING_GOVERNORS_MANOR]Governor's Manors[\\LINK] [PARAGRAPH:1][TAB][LINK=BUILDING_DUNGEON]Dungeons[\\LINK] [PARAGRAPH:1][TAB][LINK=BUILDING_PILLAR_OF_CHAINS]The Pillar of Chains[\\LINK] [PARAGRAPH:1][TAB][LINK=BUILDING_SCHOOL_OF_SADISM]The School of Sadism[\\LINK] [PARAGRAPH:1][TAB][LINK=BUILDING_PALACE_CALABIM]The Calabim Palace[\\LINK][PARAGRAPH:2]Units of this faith are more likely to gain affinity for [LINK=BONUS_MANA_BODY]Body[\\LINK] mana.[PARAGRAPH:3]The Candy Shop[PARAGRAPH:2]Sunlight filtered through the temple's stained-glass windows and cast images of angels and saints on the western wall. Within the temple parishioners shuffled uncomfortably in their pews as the Literal droned on with his sermon. The service was nearly over, and the children were even more restless than the adults. [PARAGRAPH]Amelie sat between her father and her younger brother, Artan. She was nine years old and like most of the children in the temple, was daydreaming about the many treats of the candy shop that sat in the temple's shadow. While others prayed she imagined pine nuts dipped in honey and sugar, gingerbread men and fingers sticky with pecan brittle. [PARAGRAPH]But they would only visit the candy shop if they were good during the service. It was this promise that kept the children quiet and still on Sunday mornings. It was as much a part of the children's religious experience as the Literals welcoming invocation or the brilliant arches of light that crossed the temple's dusty lofts. [PARAGRAPH]Artan swung his feet beneath the pew, risking kicking either the pew in front of him or the bottom of his seat. Amelie squeezed his hand and gave him a threatening look. Around the temple many mothers gave similar looks to their children, and Amelie's was as practiced and effective as any of them. [PARAGRAPH]The sermon closed with a final adulation and the worshipers rose to make their way out of the temple. Amelie grabbed her father's hand and Artan followed quickly behind. Now allowed to talk he was full of questions, most concerning the candy shop. They had been good so yes, they would visit the shop on the way home. And they would be able to pick one, and only one, piece of candy. It was the same as every other week, but Artan and a half-dozen other children still asked their parents if they could go, and still pleaded for more than one piece, though no parent relented. [PARAGRAPH]Amelie remained quiet. She already knew her father's answers and she was trying to pick the candy she wanted from her memory of the candy shops selection. She dreamed of caramelized pears, chewy toffee, or raisins coated with sugar and cinnamon. She carefully imagined each candy as they walked down the hill from the temple to the small shop, and changed her mind at least a dozen times. This was her favorite part of the week. Her father was sober and kind and the three of them were, for a little while, a happy family. [PARAGRAPH]The candy shop was brightly painted with a red roof and white trim over a deep green building. The front door was white, and stood beside a large window which let those on the street glimpse shelves of glass jars filled with brightly wrapped treats. The warm shop was a welcome escape from the chill wind that threatened to bring the winter's first snowfall. [PARAGRAPH]"Remember Artan, don't touch anything. " his father warned, "Pick the piece you want and tell me, so I can ask merchant Hamish to get it for us. "[PARAGRAPH]"Yes papa. " Artan answered. [PARAGRAPH]The shop's front room smelled like gingerbread and vanilla. Other families were already picking out their treats and every child carefully considered their selection, they were stuck between the desire to try a piece of candy and the fear that if they didn't look hard enough they may overlook something new and wonderful. Amelie had a different reason to spend more time picking, she didn't want this brief time in the candy shop to end. [PARAGRAPH]Merchant Hamish wandered over to Amelie as she looked at butterscotch candies in bright wrappers. He was a small man, thin and frenetic, with a weak leg that required him to use a cane. But his boundless energy made up for his clumsy three-legged gait and he made sure everyone in the shop was taken care of. He never forgot an order, and was quick to scoop, cut or measure whatever a customer requested. He wore a red and white apron that matched the color of his shop and had a crazy shock of white hair that would look unusual on anyone else, but somehow fit the shopkeepers wide, smiling face perfectly. [PARAGRAPH]"I have some ginger bread cookies I just took out of the oven. I believe you picked one last week, and nibbled on it like a mouse as you left my shop. " Hamish said. [PARAGRAPH]"I wanted it to last longer. " Amelie explained, "What are the cookies shaped like today?"[PARAGRAPH]"There were some complaints about last week's cookies. Apparently, speaker Ides didn't appreciate having his rotund figure memorialized as a cookie. And I don't know if I can afford to keep making cookies that large. So, I switched to smaller things, like buildings in the city. There are some of the cathedral bell towers, some shaped like the temple belfry, the bridge tower, the lighthouse, and the east gate. And of course. . . " Hamish added after a pause, "maybe a few fat speaker Ides hidden in the back. "[PARAGRAPH]Amelie smiled, but before she could reply another family was ready to buy and merchant Hamish was off to help them. [PARAGRAPH]Artan already had his treat, an unnaturally red candied apple. He always picked the largest treat he could find, and today was no different. He sat beside his father on a low bench in front of the window greedily devouring it. Amelie's father gave her a look that meant she had to hurry up. [PARAGRAPH]Amelie finally picked a piece of swirled vanilla and butterscotch. It was so soft she could shape it with her hands and it melted in her mouth. She made tiny bites out of the thumb sized candy as her father paid for it and they left the shop for the short walk home. [PARAGRAPH]Throughout the day Amelie continued to take tiny bites from her candy, and lied to her brother when he asked if there was any left. He finished his apple before they were out of the candy shop. By nighttime Amelie had the last bite saved in the wrapper as a treat for morning and she curled up in her blankets to sleep. [PARAGRAPH]In that grey world between sleep and wakefulness Amelie imagined a bright light in her room. She opened her eyes, but the room was dark. Her tiny bed barely fit in her room, and there was no window. The only light was from her father's room, where a lamp burned. Her door was cracked, and a sliver of pale light bled into her room from the hall. [PARAGRAPH]Amelie closed her eyes and tried to drift back to sleep. When she was again in that strange limbo before sleep the light returned. This time when she opened her eyes, the light remained. [PARAGRAPH]An angel floated above her bed. Tall and beautiful he had skin like honey and light radiated out of him as if the sun set beneath his skin. As wondrous as he appeared his face was lined with concern, and his eyes were unnaturally dark. [PARAGRAPH]Amelie pulled her blankets up above her head and curled beneath them. The angel's light shone through the thin blankets and even completely covered, Amelie could clearly see her trembling hands. But the light was mild compared to the other indications of the angel's presence. A feeling of peace and warmth radiated through the room. And though Amelie had never imagined meeting an angel before, her fear melted away. She lowered the blanket enough to see the angel's face. [PARAGRAPH]"I am truly sorry. " the angel said. His voice conveyed deep compassion and though it wasn't loud it resonated through the small house. "I tried to deliver this message to many people, but they were blind to me. "[PARAGRAPH]"Who are you?" Amelie asked. She wondered if she was dreaming, but it seemed both too real and too unlikely to be a dream. [PARAGRAPH]"I am Verchiel, herald angel of Lugus. I have come to send a message, and a warning. " the angel answered. "Your brother Artan is in great danger. He has left your house and wandered into darkness. He is already beyond my sight, and it may not be possible to save him. "[PARAGRAPH]Amelie sat up in her bed. The sudden skip and racing of her heart convinced her that she was not asleep. "Where is he? What kind of danger?" she asked. [PARAGRAPH]"He is within the home of an Anointedor, out of the light and into the province of the god of blood. " Seeing that Amelie was confused Verchiel added, "He is in the home of an evil man. That is all I know. Lugus's influence is waning and I have very little ability to act. You may be able to save your brother, you may not, or you may die in the attempt. "[PARAGRAPH]Amelie was already out of bed throwing on her shoes and coat. [PARAGRAPH]"Where is he?" Amelie asked. [PARAGRAPH]"He went to the candy shop. " Verchiel said, and for a second the angel looked afraid. Before Amelie rushed out of her room Verchiel stopped her. "I can give some small help. If you get to the temple and you hold your hand on the altar you will be safe. It is the most I can offer, though it is not much. "[PARAGRAPH]Amelie rushed out of her room and into Artan's. Just as the angel said Artan was gone. His blankets were thrown aside, and his shoes and coat were missing. Amelie went back into the hall where she noticed her room was dark again, the angel was gone. But she didn't stop to consider his disappearance as she ran into her father's room. [PARAGRAPH]Amelie's father snored loudly from his bed. His lamp flickered and cast odd shadows. The room smelled like stale beer and sweat. Amelie shouted at her father, called for him, and shook him. But in his drunken delirium he did nothing but swat her away. Amelie pushed him off the bed. He crashed to the floor in a tangle of blankets, rolled over and continued snoring. Giving up, Amelie left him and headed outside onto a small landing and down the stairs to the street. [PARAGRAPH]It was an early winter night and the season's first light snowfall was settling on the city. Amelie could see her brother's tracks in the snow and she followed them along the street toward the candy shop. [PARAGRAPH]The candy shop was only a few blocks away and it appeared as it did on all their visits. It was tidier than the surrounding buildings, and its red roof and white trim seemed at odds with the fear that clawed at Amelie as she ran. Though the front room of the shop was lost in darkness Amelie imagined that there were still rows of jars full of candy, and for a second she thought herself crazy for having rushed here in a panic. Until she noticed her brother's footprints leading up to the front porch. [PARAGRAPH]She followed them onto the porch with her heart once again racing. The roof kept the porch clear of snow, Artan's footprints ended at the porch stairs. Amelie crossed the porch, and though she had never noticed that the porch creaked in the many times she walked across it, now it seemed to cry out with each step. [PARAGRAPH]Reaching the door, she turned the knob, but it was locked. It seemed secure and untampered with. Unless someone had locked it since Artan went in, Amelie couldn't imagine how he would have gotten through. [PARAGRAPH]Walking down the porch Amelie came to the large front window. Pushing her face against the glass she looked into the room beyond. She could see the vague outlines of the room. The counter where the sales were made, the shelves covered in candy jars. What wasn't lost in darkness seemed normal. [PARAGRAPH]For the first time Amelie shivered in the cold. Though her coat was warm, she had thrown it on over her sleeping gown, which was one of her father's old shirts. It was threadbare before she got it and though it hung just past her knees, it provided little protection against the chill night air. [PARAGRAPH]Amelie walked to the end of the porch looking for anything unusual. At the very end she saw where Artan's footprints began again. He had jumped off the porch and walked around the side of the shop. [PARAGRAPH]Amelie followed the footprints around into the darkness. She had to stop and wait for her eyes to adjust, and build up her courage. There was no one on the street, but as she stood in the shop's shadow Amelie remembered that there was a guard station at the north bridge a few blocks away. If she ran there and if the guards believed her she could be back with help in fifteen, maybe twenty minutes. Deciding Artan may not be able to wait that long, she continued following the footprints around the shop. [PARAGRAPH]The back of the shop was a simple deep green wall with white trim and no doors. There was a small window set too high in the wall for Amelie to see through. But it wasn't the window or the shop that interested her, but what the tracks led to, a simple cellar door in the ground. [PARAGRAPH]The bar that usually blocked the cellar door now lay discarded beside it. Though Artan's footprints clearly led to the cellar door, there was no indication of him walking away. Amelie whispered a prayer to Lugus as she walked to the door, then hoping it was quieter than the squeaky porch, she opened it. [PARAGRAPH]Beneath the door a ladder descended into darkness. Amelie could feel warm, moist air coming out of the cellar and she was happy to see that there was a faint flickering light below. Leaning close to the opening Amelie whispered for Artan, trying to be loud enough so he would hear her, but quiet enough that someone else wouldn't. [PARAGRAPH]After waiting to see if there was any sound or response, Amelie climbed into the hole and down into the cellar. Cellars were unusual in Bourne, as the city sat between two rivers the ground was generally too wet to dig down in. Any sufficiently deep hole turned into a well. But the few she had seen had stairs leading into them, not ladders. And this ladder went down further than Amelie expected. If the candy shop had a basement, Amelie guessed that this cellar would be below it. [PARAGRAPH]The ladder ended at a hallway with a rough stone floor and slick reddish-brown walls. The ladder shaft was so small that an adult would have difficulty squeezing through, but the hallway was much larger. Looking up Amelie could see the night sky, and now that she was on the ground she could see light coming from further down the hallway. After whispering again for Artan and receiving no response she started slowly down the hallway. [PARAGRAPH]A scream echoed through the cellar so pained and shocked that Amelie nearly turned and fled back up the ladder. She couldn't imagine a person could communicate such horror in a yell, and at first thought it wasn't human. But her body went chill as she recognized the voice, it was Artan. [PARAGRAPH]Everything seemed to slow down. The smallest details leapt out at her and even though Amelie ran down the short hallway toward the screams, she felt like she was running in mud. [PARAGRAPH]The hallway continued straight with two open archways on each side. A lit lamp hung on a hook between the archways and provided the only light. Amelie only briefly glimpsed through the first two archways as she rushed by, the left contained large cages, like a kennel. Something moved in the cages, something large and covered in filth. The opposite archway was dominated by a stone table and tools and knives hung from the walls. Both rooms made Amelie more afraid. [PARAGRAPH]Amelie ran into the second room on the right, where Artan's screams were coming from. For a moment she was delighted to see that Artan stood in the middle of the room facing away from her, appearing uninjured. Amelie grabbed him and spun him around, pulling him into a hug. He jumped when she touched him and tried to push away, but as soon as he realized it was her he collapsed against her and burst into heavy, pained sobs. [PARAGRAPH]Amelie didn't have to ask why he was crying. What was hanging on the wall stole any slight relief Amelie may have felt at the recovery of her brother. [PARAGRAPH]Once it had been a man, a massively fat man buried in rolls of flesh. But all the fat had been cut away. His chest lay open, brass eyelets had been sewn into his skin and hooks held open a bloody gaping maw that exposed the bare organs beneath. His organs were tied along his spinal column and his heart still beat from within a thin mesh of netting, his lungs expanded and shrank with each breath and the slick bloody mass of organs dumped down into coiled intestines. His lower jaw and tongue had been removed so his head was a helpless hook on the wall, and his eyes moved and watched the children with an emotion Amelie had never seen before. His arms and legs had been painfully hollowed out, leaving the bone and clinging muscle, but now only air filled the loose skin. [PARAGRAPH]Amelie opened her mouth to scream but no sound came out. [PARAGRAPH]The crack of the cellar door closing shocked Amelie into action. [PARAGRAPH]"Shut up!" she yelled at Artan, "Are you okay?" As she asked, Amelie made sure Artan stayed facing her, so he couldn't see the man hanging on the wall behind him. [PARAGRAPH]Taking deep breaths Artan coughed out his words, "I. . . want. . . to. . . go. "[PARAGRAPH]"We will be okay, calm down. I am going to take you home. " Amelie said. The man hanging on the wall continued to stare at her and Amelie wondered if he had snuck down into the cellar looking for sweets too. [PARAGRAPH]The sound of another door opening and slamming closed shocked them both. It sounded closer than the cellar door. Amelie guessed it was from whatever was straight down the hallway. She could hear someone walking toward them. It was the familiar step, step, click of the candy shop owner's cane. [PARAGRAPH]Holding Artan close Amelie dove beneath the only object in the room besides the hanging man. It was a small end table near the entrance to the room. She could hear the footsteps in the hall. She held Artan's head against her chest and tried to stay quiet. She looked at the hanging man, silently pleading with him to quit staring at where they were hiding. [PARAGRAPH]The lamp light jumped and flickered as someone lifted the lamp off its hook. Then Amelie saw Hamish's wiry legs and cane step in beside the end table. She and Artan held their breath in fear. She briefly considered rushing to Hamish for help. But nothing in the candy shop owner's demeanor seemed surprised by the man hanging on the wall, and that kept Amelie trembling and quiet beneath the end table. [PARAGRAPH]Hamish set the lamp down on top of the end table, and walked across the room to look at the hanging man. With Hamish's back to them Amelie considered running from the room. As Amelie watched, Hamish set his cane down. Then he climbed up onto the hanging man and spun around, sliding his legs down into the partially hollow legs of the hanging man and his arms into the huge bodies hollow arms. Hamish's head came just under the hanging man's upper jaw, so it appeared that Hamish was wearing the hanging man's horrified face as a cruel helmet. The hanging man only offered a muffled cry in protest, but Hamish ignored him. Instead Hamish looked delighted to be settling into the hanging man's body, wearing a living man as a thick fleshy suit. The candy maker's eyes sparkled as he ran a cord through the brass eyelets, sewing himself into the body so that only his head remained exposed. [PARAGRAPH]Too terrified to see what would happen when Hamish finished sewing himself into the body Amelie crawled out from under the end table and pulled Artan behind her. She grabbed the lamp off the end table and ran into the hallway, back toward the shaft up to the cellar door. She pushed Artan in front of her and he climbed up the ladder shaft into the darkness. [PARAGRAPH]Amelie was halfway up the ladder before she heard Hamish thundering down the hallway after them. This time there was no slow click of the cane, only thick pounding footfalls. She looked down at the pudgy flesh of the hanging man's hands and arms as Hamish grabbed the ladder and shook it. But they were almost at the top. [PARAGRAPH]Artan reached the top and tried to swing the cellar door open, but it had been barred from the outside. [PARAGRAPH]"It's locked!" Artan yelled. [PARAGRAPH] The ladder shook again as Hamish was tearing it off the wall. Both the children screamed, hoping someone outside would hear them. [PARAGRAPH]"Hold onto the sides. " Amelie yelled as the ladder threatened to collapse. [PARAGRAPH]"Come down children. " Hamish called, "You will be ripe fruit on Aeron's altar tonight. "[PARAGRAPH]At that the ladder collapsed and Amelie and Artan were left braced in the shaft. Artan slipped on the wet walls. Below them Hamish laughed in delight. [PARAGRAPH]"I'm sorry. " Artan said, and his eyes began to well up with tears. [PARAGRAPH]"Listen to me. " Amelie ordered, "Is there another door?"[PARAGRAPH]"Yes," Artan said, "straight down the hallway there are stairs leading up. But that door is barred too. "[PARAGRAPH]"I don't think so, not anymore. He couldn't have barred this door from the outside if he came down it. He had to have come through the other door. " Amelie said. She could feel herself slipping. Hamish had become quiet, but he still glared up at them with hungry, depraved eyes. [PARAGRAPH]"When I say go," Amelie said, "slide down the shaft as fast as you can and run for that door. Don't stop for anything. Just run and go to the temple, not home. In the temple put your hand on the altar and don't take it off until you are safe, he can't hurt you if you touch the altar. Do you understand?"[PARAGRAPH]Artan nodded. [PARAGRAPH]Amelie took one more look down at Hamish, then she dropped the lamp. [PARAGRAPH]The lamp smashed into the hallway floor and oil splashed out in all directions, burning Hamish and setting the entire base of the shaft on fire. [PARAGRAPH]"Go!" Amelie yelled. [PARAGRAPH]Together they slid down the ladder shaft and Amelie hit the burning cellar floor hard. Artan landed on top of her and she immediately pushed him up onto his feet. The grotesque form of Hamish was slapping at itself trying to put the fire out and though he nearly filled the hallway Artan was quickly up and past him. [PARAGRAPH]Amelie slipped in the burning oil and fell when she tried to run. Her coat caught fire and when she got up again Hamish was on her. Hamish grabbed her sleeve and pulled her against the bare skin of his living suit. Amelie could feel the dank sweat of the hanging man, could smell his rotting flesh and looked up to see two pairs of eyes staring back at her. Hamish's sparkled with dark pleasure, and the hanging man looked equally horrified and helpless. [PARAGRAPH]Artan came running back down the hall. He had one of the knives from the room with the stone table in his hand. Before Hamish turned Artan leapt at him, stabbing clear through the hanging man's back and into Hamish. [PARAGRAPH]The jolt of pain was enough for Amelie to unfasten her coat and slip out of Hamish's grip. She ran by him, grabbing Artan as she went. [PARAGRAPH]They ran past the four archways to where stairs lead up into darkness. Hamish thundered down the hallway behind them and his elongated shadow was reflected on every surface by the fire burning under the ladder shaft. [PARAGRAPH]Amelie could barely make out a closed door at the top of the stairs and she prayed as she charged toward it that it would open. She hit it full speed and it flew open. [PARAGRAPH]Both children popped up into a long kitchen. A pot boiled in a fireplace, ovens were set against one wall and tables were covered with trays and bowls. A tray of freshly baked cookies sat out, half covered in green frosting. The room smelled of gingerbread and vanilla. [PARAGRAPH]Amelie and Artan were stunned by the change in scenery. The room seemed cozy and welcoming, homey in a way their home had never been. But the sound of Hamish on the stairs reminded them they were still in danger. [PARAGRAPH]"Close the door!" Amelie yelled. [PARAGRAPH]Artan swung the door closed while Amelie searched for the bar used to brace it. She found it on the counter nearest the door and raced to slide it through the four iron braces on the back of the door. It had only gone through the first when Hamish hit the door. [PARAGRAPH]For a brief second the door looked like it may hold, but then, in an explosion of splintering wood, the door flew open and Hamish came stumbling up into the kitchen. Once again, the children were off running. Artan threw open the door on the far end of the kitchen, revealing a short hallway that ended at a room the children recognized, the front room of the candy shop. [PARAGRAPH]They ran through the front room, past the shelves of jars and treats they had dreamed about for so long. Artan went to the front door, but Amelie ran to the low bench in front of the window. [PARAGRAPH]The door was locked. [PARAGRAPH]"Help me. " Amelie yelled as she lifted one end of the bench. [PARAGRAPH]Artan grabbed the other end and as Hamish entered the front room the children threw the bench through the huge front window. Amelie and Artan leapt through the shattered window behind it. [PARAGRAPH]The children screamed for help as they ran up the hill toward the temple. Hamish continued his pursuit, and though the glass shards cut up the hanging man's feet, he raced after them. If he had looked crazed in the nightmare of the cellar, he looked even more so in the ordinary city night. A man wearing another man's body as a second skin running through the streets of Bourne. [PARAGRAPH]Despite his cut feet Hamish was faster than the children on the straight road. And if anyone heard the calls for help, they weren't responding. [PARAGRAPH]By the time Amelie reached the temple, her terror had been replaced with exhaustion. She could barely breathe, she felt like she was choking, and her heart was racing so fast it made her dizzy. Amelie collapsed against the temple door and turned the handle, for the first time wondering if the temple would even be unlocked. [PARAGRAPH]It was unlocked, but where Amelie expected the warm glow of safety or an army of priests and angels set to protect them, there was only the cold, lifeless interior of the temple. The pews were empty and the stained-glass windows dark. The clear round window that formed the top of the church was the only real source of light. Through it moonlight shone down on the center of the temple, highlighting the altar. [PARAGRAPH]"Put your hand on the altar and don't let go. " Amelie commanded. [PARAGRAPH]Artan looked less convinced, his eyes widened in fear when he realized this door was the only way into, or out of, the temple. They would be trapped here. [PARAGRAPH]There was no time to try to close the door behind them, Hamish was in the doorway right behind them. As the children ran to the center of the small temple Hamish stopped to look around. It seemed that he may also have expected some sort of defense here, though he was obviously pleased to see that the temple was empty. He closed the temple door behind him. [PARAGRAPH]"For a moment I thought you might get away. " Hamish said smiling. [PARAGRAPH]Hamish stretched in the hanging man's skin, the sight was grotesque and unnerving. Bones creaked and popped and the cord binding the chest together pulled taunt against the loose flesh. Above Hamish's head the hanging man's eyes fluttered back and forth in fear as if looking for something in the room. [PARAGRAPH]"You can't hurt us here. " Amelie said, her hand firmly on the Altar's warm mahogany top. [PARAGRAPH]"Are we playing some of game?" Hamish asked, "Is this your base? I'm afraid I may have to break the rules. "[PARAGRAPH]Hamish walked down the center aisle toward them. [PARAGRAPH]"You dropped this. " Hamish said, holding out Amelie's burnt coat. [PARAGRAPH]Amelie didn't move. Artan fidgeted beside her, he still held the knife with one hand and gripped the altar with the other. [PARAGRAPH]"I'm glad you followed your brother. " Hamish said, "You will make a wonderful offering. And I can keep your brother to fatten up. "[PARAGRAPH]At that Hamish charged the altar. Just before Hamish hit them Artan let go of the altar and stepped in front of his sister, swinging wildly with his knife. [PARAGRAPH]"No!" Amelie screamed, grabbing Artan's free hand. [PARAGRAPH]Hamish caught Artan's knife hand in a fist so large it nearly covered up Artan's entire forearm. Hamish pushed down on the back of Artan's hand with his thumb, forcing him to drop the knife, then with a sickening pop Artan's wrist broke. [PARAGRAPH]Artan screamed in pain. And though Amelie pulled, her brother was caught between them. [PARAGRAPH]Hamish towered over the children, Amelie barely came to his waist and as Hamish pulled the screaming Artan away, Amelie's finger slid easily across the smooth altar. She stretched as her fingers came to the edge of the altar. Just as her fingertips were all that was left of her contact with the altar she let go of Artan's hand. [PARAGRAPH]"Shhh. . . " Hamish whispered to the crying boy. Hamish lifted Artan, placing one meaty hand over Artan's mouth. Three sets of eyes looked down at Amelie, but she stood unmoving, hand once again firmly on the altar. Tears silently pouring down her face. [PARAGRAPH]Without saying anything Hamish turned and walked out of the temple, carrying the struggling Artan with him. When he was gone Amelie collapsed beside the altar, with her hand still on it, and cried so violently that her small body shook with each wail and ragged coughing breath. That is where they found her the next morning, though Hamish and Artan were gone.\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\t\r\n[PARAGRAPH:3]Priests and Demons of [LINK=RELIGION_ANOINTED]The Anointed[\\LINK] will have their ardas most strengthened by the influence of [LINK=RELIGION_ANOINTED]The Anointed[\\LINK], but also strengthened to a lesser degree by [LINK=RELIGION_COUNCIL_OF_ESUS]The Council of Esus[\\LINK], [LINK=RELIGION_COVEN]The Between[\\LINK], [LINK=RELIGION_WHITE_HAND]The White Hand[\\LINK], [LINK=RELIGION_STEWARDS_OF_INEQUITY]The Stewards of Inequity[\\LINK], [LINK=RELIGION_THE_ASHEN_VEIL]The Ashen Veil[\\LINK], [LINK=RELIGION_EMBER_LEGION]The Ember Legion[\\LINK], and [LINK=RELIGION_SONS_OF_DISCORD]The Sons of Discord[\\LINK]. Its arda will be weakened by [LINK=RELIGION_CHILDREN_OF_THE_ONE]The Children of The One[\\LINK], [LINK=RELIGION_MATRONAE]The Matronae[\\LINK], [LINK=RELIGION_BROTHERHOOD_OF_WARDENS]The Brotherhood of Wardens[\\LINK], [LINK=RELIGION_GREY_COUNCIL]The Grey Council[\\LINK], [LINK=RELIGION_HOUSE_OF_PLENTY]The House of Plenty[\\LINK], [LINK=RELIGION_UNBLEMISHED]The Unblemished[\\LINK], [LINK=RELIGION_RINGGIVER]The Ringgivers[\\LINK], [LINK=RELIGION_THE_EMPYREAN]The Empyrean[\\LINK], the [LINK=RELIGION_RUNES_OF_KILMORPH]The Runes of Kilmorph[\\LINK], [LINK=RELIGION_CULT_OF_THE_DRAGON]Cult of the Dragon[\\LINK], [LINK=RELIGION_LAERAN_CORD]The Laeran Cord[\\LINK], [LINK=RELIGION_THE_EMPYREAN]The Order[\\LINK], and [LINK=RELIGION_FOXMEN]The Foxmen[\\LINK]. It will be weakened in Good and strengthened in Evil lands. It will be strengthened by proximity to [LINK=BONUS_MANA_BODY]Body Mana[\\LINK] and weakened by [LINK=BONUS_MANA_SPIRIT]Spirit Mana[\\LINK]. It is strenthened near the [LINK=IMPROVEMENT_GRIGI_ABATTOIR]Grigi Abattoir[\\LINK], [LINK=IMPROVEMENT_HELLFIRE]Hellfire[\\LINK], [LINK=IMPROVEMENT_ODIOS_PRISON]Odio's Prison[\\LINK], and [LINK=FEATURE_TORMENTED_SOULS]Tormented Souls[\\LINK], and weakened on [LINK=FEATURE_HALLOWED_GROUND]Hallowed Ground[\\LINK]. \r\n	
Magister_CIV4GameText_FFH2.xml	TXT_KEY_RELIGION_FOXMEN_PEDIA	The Foxmen are a cult of Tali, God of the Wind. He has no formal priesthood but many shrines built in the most remote and dangerous parts of Erebus. Some say the Foxmen put them there just to give each other excuses to travel. The Foxmen are renowned for their Wanderlust. They cannot stand to stay still for very long. There are no better explorers than their Drifters, nor better story tellers. The closest thing to a mass religious service are the convocations that they Foxmen are known to hold on the rooftops of popular inns, where they regale each other with tall tales of their past or future exploits. [PARAGRAPH:2]State religion allows access to:[PARAGRAPH:1][TAB][LINK=UNIT_PRIEST_FOXMEN]Drifter [\\LINK] (unit)[PARAGRAPH:1][TAB][LINK=UNIT_HIGH_PRIEST_FOXMEN]Wind Dancers[\\LINK] (unit)[PARAGRAPH:1][TAB][LINK=UNIT_WIND_KNIGHT]Wind Knights [\\LINK] (unit)[PARAGRAPH:1][TAB]Halves the production cost of: [PARAGRAPH:1][TAB][LINK=BUILDING_HUNTING_LODGE]Hunting Lodges[\\LINK] [PARAGRAPH:1][TAB][LINK=BUILDING_CARNIVAL]Carnivals[\\LINK] [PARAGRAPH:1][TAB][LINK=BUILDING_STABLE]Stables[\\LINK] [PARAGRAPH:1][TAB][LINK=BUILDING_ADVENTURERS_GUILD]Adventurers' Guilds[\\LINK] [PARAGRAPH:1][TAB][LINK=BUILDING_TAVERN]Taverns[\\LINK] [PARAGRAPH:1][TAB][LINK=BUILDING_TAVERN_GRIGORI]Grigori Taverns[\\LINK] [PARAGRAPH:1][TAB][LINK=BUILDING_INN]Inns[\\LINK] [PARAGRAPH:1][TAB][LINK=BUILDING_TOWER_OF_THE_ELEMENTS]The Tower of the Elements[\\LINK] [PARAGRAPH:1][TAB][LINK=BUILDING_SLYPH_SEARCH]The Slyph Search[\\LINK] [PARAGRAPH:1][TAB][LINK=BUILDING_PALACE_HIPPUS]The Hippus Palace[\\LINK] [PARAGRAPH:2]Units of this faith are more likely to gain affinity for [LINK=BONUS_MANA_AIR]Air[\\LINK] mana\r\n\t\t\r\n\t\t\r\n[PARAGRAPH:3]Priests and Angels of [LINK=RELIGION_FOXMEN]The Foxmen[\\LINK] will have their ardas most strengthened by the influence of [LINK=RELIGION_FOXMEN]The Foxmen[\\LINK], but also strengthened to a lesser degree by [LINK=RELIGION_HOUSE_OF_PLENTY]The House of Plenty[\\LINK], [LINK=RELIGION_FELLOWSHIP_OF_LEAVES]The Fellowship of the Leaves[\\LINK], [LINK=RELIGION_OCTOPUS_OVERLORDS]The Undertow[\\LINK], [LINK=RELIGION_COUNCIL_OF_ESUS]The Council of Esus[\\LINK], and [LINK=RELIGION_SONS_OF_DISCORD]The Sons of Discord[\\LINK]. Its arda will be weakened by [LINK=RELIGION_CHILDREN_OF_THE_ONE]The Children of The One[\\LINK], [LINK=RELIGION_MATRONAE]The Matronae[\\LINK], the [LINK=RELIGION_RUNES_OF_KILMORPH]The Runes of Kilmorph[\\LINK], [LINK=RELIGION_GREY_COUNCIL]The Grey Council[\\LINK], [LINK=RELIGION_THE_EMPYREAN]The Order[\\LINK], [LINK=RELIGION_THE_ASHEN_VEIL]The Ashen Veil[\\LINK], [LINK=RELIGION_WHITE_HAND]The White Hand[\\LINK], [LINK=RELIGION_CULT_OF_THE_DRAGON]Cult of the Dragon[\\LINK], [LINK=RELIGION_COVEN]The Between[\\LINK], [LINK=RELIGION_EMBER_LEGION]The Ember Legion[\\LINK], and [LINK=RELIGION_ANOINTED]The Anointed[\\LINK]. It will be strengthened in Neutral lands. It will be strengthened by proximity to [LINK=BONUS_MANA_AIR]Air Mana[\\LINK], the [LINK=IMPROVEMENT_MAELSTROM]Maelstrom[\\LINK], [LINK=IMPROVEMENT_WINDMILL]Windmills[\\LINK], and every sort of explorable lair. It weakened by [LINK=IMPROVEMENT_IMPROVEMENT_CAGE]Cages[\\LINK], [LINK=BONUS_MANA_EARTH]Earth Mana[\\LINK], and by having fortified too long in one place. \r\n	
Magister_CIV4GameText_FFH2.xml	TXT_KEY_RELIGION_COVEN_PEDIA	Ceridwen, Goddess of the Stars, of Magic,and of the Social Relationships that bind mortals together, is served by numerous cults with only tenuous ties to one another. [PARAGRAPH:2]Ceridwen first appeared to a suicidal Kylorin, asking him to worship her in exchange for arcane knowledge and the power to resurrect his wife indefinitely so they might restart their marriage untainted by her adultery. He attempted to make her cult into the State Religion of Patria, but only a small group of elites ever accepted her tenets. The most elite of the elites were the Coven of the Black Candle, whom she tasked with blotting out The Sun. This Coven consisted of Kylorin himself, Os-Gabella, Asmoday, Faeryl Viconia, Alexis, Barbatos, and "Wode"-who was actually the God of Shadow masquerading as the mortal master of Shadow magic. Esus transformed his Seven Aspects (the greatest of his True Angels besides Iaegus) into the Masks each Coven member wore, so that no power in creation could detect or control them. The members could never learn each other's identity, as the animus between Asmoday and the Fey would have made their cooperation impossible. [PARAGRAPH:1]A lesser Masked Coven, called "The Between," continues to this day. It is a small religion whose members are mostly sorceresses whose spirits, minds and occasionally bodies explore worlds beyond Erebus. Many are driven mad by the experience. They refer to their goddesses as the Maiden of the Mask. [PARAGRAPH:1]Among the Sheaim there is a cult filled with a missionary zeal to reach all nations and bring their best and brightest back to learn sorcery without the strictures and limitations placed on adepts by mage guilds in all other lands. They promise unfettered access to secret knowledge of all sorts, but tend to steer their students into the darkest of arts. The Emrys have a goal more grand that blotting our the son. They want to bring on Armageddon and end Erebus entirely. [PARAGRAPH:1]Not all of her worshipers are that evil. The Chainbreakers have more noble goals. They believe the goddess is best served by breaking unjust social bonds. They hate any relationship that is not entirely consensual. Some try to destroy marriages and turn children against their parents, but most focus their radical activism on fighting for the abolition of slavery. They can always be counted upon to provide aid and sanctuary to runaway slaves, but prefer to give them arms and help them slay their former masters. [PARAGRAPH:2]State religion allows access to:[PARAGRAPH:1][TAB][LINK=UNIT_GOSEA]Gosea the Dwindling[\\LINK] (world unit)[PARAGRAPH:1][TAB][LINK=UNIT_CHAINBREAKER]Chainbreaker [\\LINK] (unit)[PARAGRAPH:1][TAB][LINK=UNIT_EMRY]Emrys [\\LINK] ([LINK=CIVILIZATION_SHEAIM]Sheaim[\\LINK] unique unit)[PARAGRAPH:1][TAB]Halves the production cost of: [PARAGRAPH:1][TAB][LINK=BUILDING_PLANAR_GATE]Planar Gates[\\LINK] [PARAGRAPH:1][TAB][LINK=BUILDING_OBSIDIAN_GATE]Obsidian Gates[\\LINK] [PARAGRAPH:1][TAB][LINK=BUILDING_PROPHECY_OF_RAGNAROK]The Prophecy of Ragnarok[\\LINK] [PARAGRAPH:1][TAB][LINK=BUILDING_THE_NEXUS]The Nexus[\\LINK] [PARAGRAPH:1][TAB][LINK=BUILDING_PALACE_SHEAIM]The Sheaim Palace[\\LINK][PARAGRAPH:2]Units of this faith are more likely to gain affinity for [LINK=BONUS_MANA_DIMENSIONAL]Dimensional[\\LINK] mana\r\n[PARAGRAPH:3]Priests and Demons of  [LINK=RELIGION_COVEN]The Between[\\LINK] will have its arda most strengthened by the influence of the [LINK=RELIGION_COVEN]The Between[\\LINK], but also strengthened to a lesser degree by [LINK=RELIGION_THE_ASHEN_VEIL]The Ashen Veil[\\LINK], [LINK=RELIGION_STEWARDS_OF_INEQUITY]The Stewards of Inequity[\\LINK], [LINK=RELIGION_ANOINTED]The Anointed[\\LINK], [LINK=RELIGION_SONS_OF_DISCORD]The Sons of Discord[\\LINK], Ember Legion, [LINK=RELIGION_COUNCIL_OF_ESUS]The Council of Esus[\\LINK], [LINK=RELIGION_WHITE_HAND]The White Hand[\\LINK], and [LINK=RELIGION_OCTOPUS_OVERLORDS]The Undertow[\\LINK]. Its arda will be weakened by [LINK=RELIGION_CHILDREN_OF_THE_ONE]The Children of The One[\\LINK], [LINK=RELIGION_MATRONAE]The Matronae[\\LINK], [LINK=RELIGION_RINGGIVER]The Ringgivers[\\LINK], [LINK=RELIGION_BROTHERHOOD_OF_WARDENS]The Brotherhood of Wardens[\\LINK], [LINK=RELIGION_GREY_COUNCIL]The Grey Council[\\LINK], [LINK=RELIGION_THE_EMPYREAN]The Empyrean[\\LINK], [LINK=RELIGION_HOUSE_OF_PLENTY]The House of Plenty[\\LINK], [LINK=RELIGION_UNBLEMISHED]The Unblemished[\\LINK], [LINK=RELIGION_THE_EMPYREAN]The Order[\\LINK], [LINK=RELIGION_LAERAN_CORD]The Laeran Cord[\\LINK], [LINK=RELIGION_ETERNAL_CABAL]The Eternal Cabal[\\LINK], [LINK=RELIGION_CULT_OF_THE_DRAGON]Cult of the Dragon[\\LINK], the [LINK=RELIGION_RUNES_OF_KILMORPH]The Runes of Kilmorph[\\LINK], and [LINK=RELIGION_FELLOWSHIP_OF_LEAVES]The Fellowship of the Leaves[\\LINK], and [LINK=RELIGION_FOXMEN]The Foxmen[\\LINK]. It will be weakened in Good or Neutral and strengthened in Evil lands. It will be strengthened by proximity to [LINK=BONUS_MANA_DIMENSIONAL]Dimensional Mana[\\LINK] and weakened by [LINK=BONUS_MANA_ENCHANTMENT]Enchantment Mana[\\LINK]. It is strenthened near the [LINK=IMPROVEMENT_TAPESTRY_HOUSE]Tapestry House[\\LINK], [LINK=IMPROVEMENT_PORTAL]Portals[\\LINK], [LINK=IMPROVEMENT_HELLFIRE]Hellfire[\\LINK], and [LINK=FEATURE_TORMENTED_SOULS]Tormented Souls[\\LINK], and weakened on [LINK=FEATURE_HALLOWED_GROUND]Hallowed Ground[\\LINK] and [LINK=IMPROVEMENT_RING_OF_WARDING]Rings of Warding[\\LINK]. 	
Magister_CIV4GameText_FFH2.xml	TXT_KEY_RELIGION_HOUSE_OF_PLENTY	House of Plenty	
